[82.983131, "o", "  Temporally multiplexed ion-photon quantum interface via fast ion-chain transport\r\n\r\n                             Bingran You,1, 2 Qiming Wu,1, 2, ∗ David Miron,1, 2 Wenjun Ke,1\r\n                            Inder Monga,2 Erhan Saglamyurek,1, 2 and Hartmut Haeffner1, 2, †\r\n                       1\r\n                           Department of Physics, University of California, Berkeley, CA 94270, USA\r\n                              2\r\n                                Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory, Berkeley, CA 94720, USA\r\n                                                   (Dated: January 12, 2026)\r\n                High-rate remote entanglement between photon and matter-based qubits is essential for dis-\r\n             tributed quantum information processing. A key technique to increase the modest entangling rates\r\n             of existing long-distance quantum networking approaches is multiplexing. Here, we demonstrate\r\n             a temporally multiplexed ion-photon interface via rapid transport of a chain of nine calcium ions\r\n             across 74 µm within 86 µs. The non-classical nature of the multiplexed photons is verified by mea-\r\n             suring the second-order correlation function with an average value of g (2) (0) = 0.060(13), indicating\r\n             negligible crosstalk between the multiplexed modes. In addition, we characterize the motional\r\n             degree-of-freedom of the ion crystal after transport and find that it is coherently excited to as much\r\n             as n̄α ≈ 110 for the center-of-mass mode. Our proof-of-principle implementation paves the way\r\n             for large-scale quantum networking with trapped ions, but highlights some challenges that must be\r\n             overcome.\r\n\r\n\r\n   Remote entanglement across distant quantum nodes [?            light at convenient wavelengths for quantum frequency\r\n? ] may be used for long-distance quantum key distribu-           conversion (QFC) [? ? ], and the possibility of long-\r\ntion [? ? ], modular quantum computer architectures [?            lived storage of entanglement [? ? ]. On the other hand,\r\n? ], as well as quantum enhanced metrology and sens-              implementing a multiplexed light-matter interface with\r\ning [? ? ? ]. Light-matter quantum interfaces are a fun-          these systems is technically challenging. Towards over-\r\ndamental building block for such applications, and allow          coming this problem, a few multiplexing schemes have\r\nfor distributed entanglement between stationary matter            already been proposed for ion and atom-based quantum\r\nqubits by using \"flying\" photons.                                 processors [? ? ? ? ]. The only reported experimental\r\n   For practical purposes, these quantum interfaces need          work, we are aware of, is the demonstration of multi-\r\nto be capable of establishing remote entanglement at              plexing using a static three-ion chain [? ]. In view of the\r\nhigh rates across a large-scale network of quantum nodes.         recent advances of the quantum CCD architecture [? ? ?\r\nHowever, in widely adopted probabilistic schemes based            ], a complementary approach to multiplexing is the pro-\r\non heralded photon detection [? ? ? ], it is infeasible to        cess of ion-transport through a specific spatial location\r\ndistribute entanglement at useful rates over a few kilo-          with maximized photon coupling efficiency.\r\nmeters, as the attempt rate is limited by the round-trip             In this work, we demonstrate a temporal multiplexing\r\ntravel-time of photons in a single mode. For example, for         scheme based on the transport of an ion-chain for improv-\r\na communication distance of 10 km, photon-travel time             ing the rate of ion-photon entanglement over long dis-\r\nwill be around 100 µs, meaning that the attempt can-              tances. In our experiments, we generate on-demand sin-\r\n"]
